  },
  "interval": "an arm's interval is the range of its two boots' medians (20 calls each); 'faster'/'slower' = the candidate's two boots wholly below/above the base's two"
 },
 "verdict": "pass",
 "reason": "Cold-miss lookups are faster beyond the interval at M16 (two-plane 0.909x, packed 0.934x) and M32 (0.897x, 0.925x), and Zipfian lookups at M16, M48 and M128 in both cache modes (0.961-0.976x) and M4 packed (0.968x); hot-cache steps are faster at 15 of 16 cells (0.89-0.95x). The remaining cache cells (cold M4 both modes 0.963-0.972x, Zipfian M4 two-plane 0.981x, hot M4 two-plane 0.953x) improve inside the interval. No cell in any of the four modes is slower (60 cells: 48 faster, 12 within). Rows are identical across arms and modes, the 52 engram-row tests pass on both boots, and the same-session ABBA replaces the recorded-0750 comparison that parked the PR.",
 "notes": [
  "With the PR's single call per boot (rep 1 only), 5 of 60 cells read 'slower' (Zipfian M128 two_plane_cache64k; hot M4 two_plane_nocache and packed_nocache; hot M8 and M16 packed_nocache), 32 'within' and 23 'faster'. Each is one boot's first call landing high (e.g. hot M4 two_plane_nocache 56.41 us on c52 boot1 against 34.56 on c52 boot2 and 33.98-34.36 on base; Zipfian M128 57.41 on c52 boot2 against 40.36 on boot1): one call is 6-60 wall-clock steps, so its spread is wider than the effect. With 20 calls a boot those five are 2 'faster' and 3 'within', none slower. Both views are in measured.",
  "hot/M1/two_plane_nocache is the first timed cell of every call and swings 46-73 us across boots (cold start of the call); it is 'within'.",
  "The PR README's table has cold-miss and Zipfian M=1 rows that neither its receipts nor the bench contain (cold M is 4,16,32 and Zipfian M 4,16,48,128); the comparison here uses the bench's rows.",
  "The bench times host stage + GPU gather by wall clock (perf_counter) over the disk reader's io_uring path, a host and I/O path rather than a decode GPU kernel, so CUDA-graph replay does not apply; the reader runs O_DIRECT reads from an ext4 directory on sp8's NVMe root."
 ]
}